
                       1988 FIG Convention
                         Fire Side Chat
                        by Charles Moore
                    Reported by Dennis Ruffer
 
     This report can not be considered a transcript of Chuck's
talk, since I am merely attempting to reconstruct it from the
notes I took during his talk.  I do hope however, that this
report conveys the intent of his words, and that by reading this
report, the rest of you can get a feeling for the current state
of our industry.
 
     It is not quite clear why this is called the "fire side"
chat, since they have never had a fire.  Chuck wore his
Australian hat, since he did not want to look too much like
Einstein in the advertisements for this convention.  He got it at
this last year's Australian Symposium.  In his travels, he finds
it very remarkable that there is a community of interest in Forth
around the world.
 
     On November 19, 1968 the first Forth computer said OK.  It
was an IBM 1130 computer located in a warehouse in Amsterdam, NY. 
It was being designed as a pattern designer for carpets, but
since its black and white display was totally inappropriate for
the job, it was abandoned within a year.  It is very remarkable
how similar that early Forth was to the Forth that we have today.
 
     Chuck really enjoyed the "Real-Time Programmers Contest"
that we had this year.  As he walked around the room, he noticed
how everyone was using very interactive modular designs.  He
wondered if the ones who used multiple windowed Forth found that
those tools got in the way, or if the ones who did not have those
tools missed them (he paused but there were no answers).  He
suggests that scrolling the parameters for this project would
work very nicely with his 3 key keyboard.  He was questioned if
he would use the display techniques from the contest in his next
computer, but laughing he said "no, it has a moving part and no
color".  Although he did not enter this years contest, using the
excuse that he had no computer to show off, he will try it next
year.
 
     Although he designed the Novix with a 16 bit "B" port that
was intended to be a buss for interconnecting multiple CPU's, he
has never seen the technique used.  So far, he has not seen
anything that he could not do with just one Novix.  It is not
clear what are computers, or what is software.  All you need is a
couple of latches and shift registers.  Minimalist to the core. 
He is working on another chip, but he is having to learn how to
use workstations to enter the design.
 
     Forth, Incorporated is a neat company, and the people are
getting down to real programs.  However, they keep reinventing
the wheel, doing the same applications over again.  CAD existed
many years ago, but few remember how it was done.  The 300 foot
telescope that crumbled in Green Bay, was written in Forth.
 
     Forth should be under 4 K bytes, but he hasn't found a lower
limit.  You don't need a neural net or adaptive program to
balance a broom.  All you need is a clever programmer, but they
are in short supply.  He urges the neural net people to show him
something significant they have accomplished.  Just because you
can not see the inner workings of a system does not mean that it
is chaotic, but merely non-linear.
 
     The ANSII standard effort is using a ridiculous amount of
energy.  It behooves us all to see that it is a good standard. 
It is not easy to standardize the ground work of Forth.  He
considers the "little user" the mainstay of Forth, and they are
not well represented.  For those who it affects (authors, etc.),
they must make sure they are not disappointed.  "GET INVOLVED!"
The future of Forth looks very promising.
 
     He just got done with the Science Fiction book, "The
Regiment", about a war being fought by mercenaries.  They don't
really care if they are killed.  The goal is to fight well.  We
tend to get too serious.  The reason Forth is spreading is that
it is fun.  He recently saw a book about Forth in Russian, it's
in short supply.  Starting Forth is being translated to Russian,
and has already been translated in Portuguese.
 
     1/2 to 1 billion dollars have been invested in Forth over
the past 10 years.  Maybe some entrepreneur would like to invest
1/2 a million more.  He will get good results.  Harris has
invested more than anyone has to date, and they are doing us all
a great service using the "F" word.  If they sell a lot of chips
to people who don't care about Forth, they will help us a lot. 
Despite the rumor of their demise, Novix is still in business,
but they are for sale and still have chips in stock.
 
     At this point, Chuck was done with his rambling notes, and
he opened the discussion to questions from the floor.  Although I
wasn't able to hear all the questions, the following are some of
his thoughts that the questions brought out.
 
     The high cost of RAM is only a temporary problem, and soon
they will again tend toward zero.  However, people are not happy
with the gigabyte operating systems, and they often forget the
other extraneous cost of memory.  Memory will always be the
highest cost item in a system.  Chuck believes in distributed
systems, where each piece only takes 1 or 2 programmers.  Every
large project is conceived by management.  His concept is to have
all the programmers in the same room.  You will loose some effort
in socializing, but you will also get the most work done.  He
believes the documentation should be written in parallel with the
programming and use the same interactive techniques.
 
     What is Forth, a tool to use a machine, or to share
algorithms?  Chuck suggested to the ANS committee that the
standard be a publication standard, that might give us a more
effective way to share programs, but people could not just type
in the programs.  He noted that all the contestants were doing
the same thing, each in his own way.  He invented something that
he could just hack at his computer.  There is nothing that Forth
can not do, and it is superior in every application.  It is
impossible to define Forth, but it is that which we have in
common.  He no longer wants his computer to understand his voice,
since he found that he has nothing he wants to say to it.  He was
filmed at this years Hackers Convention (because he had a red
shirt on).  The reporters who were called in were from a local
CBS station who called them all criminals.  He urges us all to
boycott CBS to object to their biased opinions.


